::ENG4_U02_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Passage: At dusk, Leona touched the old harbor bell. Its thin sound crossed the water and came back changed. What is the functional significance of the bell? {
~A. It only tells the exact time of day.#The passage does not use it as a clock.
=B. It connects Leona's return to memory and suggests that the past comes back altered.#Correct. This explains the bell's function.
~C. It proves the harbor is dangerous.#The warning signs do not make the bell's function danger.
~D. It has no connection to Leona's memory.#The bell clearly connects to memory and return.
}

::ENG4_U02_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Leona wonders whether leaving was betrayal or another way of carrying home with her. Which theme is most supported? {
~A. People should never leave home.#This is too absolute.
~B. Harbors always represent failure.#This is unsupported.
~C. Memory prevents all change.#The sound comes back changed.
=D. Home can remain part of a person even after physical departure.#Correct. The passage complicates leaving and belonging.
}

::ENG4_U02_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: Leona first sees the harbor as a place she left, but then wonders if leaving was another way of carrying home. What shift occurs? {
=A. Her perspective moves from guilt toward a more complex understanding of belonging.#Correct. She reconsiders what leaving means.
~B. Her perspective changes from joy to hatred.#There is no evidence of hatred.
~C. She stops thinking about home completely.#She thinks deeply about home.
~D. She decides memory has no value.#The passage values memory.
}

::ENG4_U02_L08_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: The bell's sound was "thin, almost embarrassed." What is the effect of this figurative description? {
~A. It gives the bell literal human emotions only.#The description is figurative.
~B. It makes the harbor seem loud and triumphant.#Thin and embarrassed suggest the opposite.
=C. It gives the setting a hesitant, emotionally complicated mood.#Correct. The personification supports Leona's uncertainty.
~D. It removes mood from the passage.#The phrase creates mood.
}

::ENG4_U02_L08_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.W.01] Passage: A student writes a narrative about returning home after years away. Which choice best applies Unit 2 narrative craft? {
~A. List every street in the town with no conflict.#This lacks narrative focus.
=B. Use a concrete object from home to reveal conflict, memory, and a universal theme.#Correct. This applies literary craft to narrative writing.
~C. State the theme directly before any scene begins.#This tells rather than develops theme.
~D. Avoid character perspective so the story is neutral.#Perspective is necessary.
}
